foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Friday January 13th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  5G for years it's been billed as the key  to faster cell phone connections not  only that it was supposed to usher in a  world of connected possibilities for  technology 5G powered cars that notify  traffic lights when you're close 5G  powered robots performing surgeries or  preparing food but if you're feeling  like 5G Tech is it living up to all  those promises you're not alone  we've got our senior personal Tech  columnist Joanna stern here to explain  why and what you can do about it hey  Joanna Hey Zoe So Joanna maybe we should  start with some background why did we  expect 5G to be such a game changer  because the carriers made us think that  it was going to be the next coming of  Technology  Gods well carriers do this every time  they have a big Network upgrade but 5G  was particularly  just blown out right you had Verizon  with major ads you still see them  everywhere the 5G America is waiting for  T-Mobile had this crazy Super Bowl ad  with Miley Cyrus and Dolly Parton 5G  phones  then you had a t where they were saying  it's going to transform the future and  so there was just tons of marketing hype  about 5G and many people also remember  the race to 5G that we have to beat  other countries to 5G and it wasn't  really clear what 5G was going to do for  us as consumers but then we started  getting 5G phones and so they put the  focus there right you buy these 5G  phones you're gonna get blazing fast  speeds okay but a lot of us have 5G now  on our phones and it doesn't feel like  it's living up to its promises so why is  that well it depends on what network or  Carrier you have but the real reason is  is that 5G is not really meant for  phones 4G unlocked a ton of things that  we now do on our phones and made it  really seamless streaming Netflix Uber  because location services got better and  the data connection was faster Tick Tock  Instagram all these things loaded faster  on 4G and the mobile apps they were  built for these types of networks to  give better performance with lower  bandwidth and so now 5G comes along and  it's like trying to put out a t candle  with a huge fire hose you don't need  that much power on your phones so can  you explain a little bit you know what  do we need to know about how 5G works  just to make it a little bit clearer for  what we're getting on our phones and  maybe what we don't need the biggest  confusion about 5G has really been that  all 5G is created equal and so what  happened in the beginning of 5G really  is that really a lot of 4G became 5G and  so when your indicator on your phone  says 5G and you're expecting Crazy Fast  speeds well you might just be getting  something very similar to 4G in fact I  have Verizon I often just see the  regular 5G logo when I turn off 5G and  I'm back on LTE I really don't even see  the speed difference and don't feel that  difference so that was something called  Low band 5G then the real promise is  something called mid-band 5G and this is  sort of The Sweet Spot between the low  and the high obviously but it's The  Sweet Spot because it covers more space  and it has faster speeds than 4G  the issue is is that not all the  networks all the carriers had access to  this band of 5G T-Mobile has a lot of  this band because of their acquisition  of Sprint a few years back and so if  you're a T-Mobile customer you actually  really may see the benefit of 5G and  then there's this high band 5G which is  called millimeter wave it's less  frequent it's super fast but you've  pretty much got to be near a tower or in  one of these public spots where  millimeter wave 5G is available like a  airport or an arena so for people who  maybe made the switch to 5G and who are  possibly you know paying more to have it  what can they do to to get the most out  of it so number one thing is to just  look at your carrier right because even  if you have a 5G phone you may not be  actually getting the best 5G service and  so contact your carrier or look at the  plans my editor I'll call them out here  he thought oh I have five Gene he said I  don't see the 5G Ultra wide band  indicator you've been talking about  which is what what Verizon Brands it's  mid-band or high band 5G but he wasn't  paying for the plan that had the faster  network access then he started paying  for the plan and he started to see the  5G UW so the carriers will actually tell  you in the network indicator if you have  the mid band or high band connection on  T-Mobile it will say 5G UC on Verizon it  will say 5G UW and on at T it will say  5G Plus  because things were not complicated  enough  have the carriers said anything about  this I mean are we hearing from them at  all about what people should expect or  why 5G isn't as blazing fast as we maybe  thought it might be yeah so at T and  Verizon both are working on that build  out of that mid-band Network which  should make things better but they  ultimately also will point to the fact  that 5G has made some big difference in  some different areas one area is home  internet you can get a 5G router if  you're on Verizon or T-Mobile and that  means you can get these really fast  speeds at home when maybe you had an  older type of connection at home maybe a  cable and also you also can now deal  with the carriers versus using some  older cable company so that's one place  it has made a difference another place  that they'll point to and analysts  pointed to when I spoke to them was  during these points of heavy congestion  on the network say you're on a train and  everyone's on their phone say you're in  an arena and everybody's on their phone  5G gives you a higher data capacity so  so that may lessen the burden on you and  you may notice that things upload faster  or download faster I  in 5G isn't really for phones but when  do we expect that 5G might live up to  the connectivity promises that so many  people talked about  so the thing that the carriers and the  analysts told me is that a lot of the  promise of 5G comes with latency lower  latency latency is the time it takes for  data to travel from one point to another  right so the faster that connection the  more things can happen instantaneously  and that can help a lot with new  categories so drones self-driving cars  things that are not your phone  specifically we've been hearing about in  5G you know these robot surgeons you'll  have a surgeon in Miami who could  perform the surgery using all types of  computers and Technology where the robot  is let's say you're in New York right  and so you want that robot to be on 5G  or to have that low latency because you  wouldn't want it to take longer you  wouldn't want something to be not  instantaneous during surgery so all of  these things really depend on that  latency getting better which is going to  get better as these networks get better  so something to uh to wait for and  anticipate that's our senior personal  technology columnist Joanna Stern thanks  for joining us Joanna thank you  and that's it for Tech news briefing  this week our producer is Julie Chang  our supervising producer is Melanie Roy  our executive producer is Chris sinsley  and I'm your host Zoe Thomas a quick  heads up we'll be off on Monday in honor  of MLK day but we'll be back on Tuesday  with more Tech news thanks for listening  and have a great weekend  [Music]  thank you

